  "findings": [
    {
      "description": "The request XML is parsed with xml.dom.minidom.parseString on untrusted input without disabling DTD/external entity processing. This is vulnerable to XXE and entity expansion (billion laughs) attacks, which can lead to local file disclosure, SSRF, or denial of service. Static scanners did not flag it, but manual review confirms the unsafe use of the standard library XML parser.",
      "category": "injection",
      "severity": "high"
    },
    {
      "description": "No exception handling around XML parsing; malformed or malicious non-XML request bodies cause an unhandled parse exception and return a 500 without a controlled error response. This is a robustness/availability issue on a public import endpoint.",
      "category": "logic-bug",
      "severity": "low"
    }
  ],
  "decision": "HOLD",
  "fix_description": "Fix 1 (high, injection/XXE): replace xml.dom.minidom.parseString with a safe XML parser that disables DTD and external entity processing, e.g. defusedxml.minidom.parseString if available, or configure minidom's underlying parser with an EntityResolver that rejects DTDs and set XML_PARSE_NOENT/DTDLOAD off. Fix 2 (low, robustness): wrap parse_shipping_feed/import_feed in try/except for XML/parse errors and return a controlled error response such as {'error': 'invalid XML'}, 400 instead of an unhandled 500. Both fixes are local to parse_shipping_feed and import_feed and can be applied safely; the high-severity fix should be reviewed before merge, but it is fixable within the current PR head branch without changing public API.",
